  "steps": [
    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s01",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.inner_join"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "INNER JOIN",
      "title": "Combining two tables on a shared key",
      "lede": "The customers table holds names; the orders table holds delivery times. To see both together, you join them.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s01.b1",
          "role": "intro",
          "text": "QuickCart stores customer names in a customers table and delivery times in a separate orders table. Each orders row carries a customer_id that points back to customers. To see a customer's name alongside their delivery times, you need both tables combined into one result — that's what a JOIN does.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join#tb1"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join#f1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s01.b2",
          "role": "definition",
          "text": "For each row of T1, an INNER JOIN produces a joined row for each row of T2 that satisfies the join condition; rows from either table with no match are dropped. The query SELECT c.name, o.delivery_time_minutes FROM customers AS c JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id pairs every customer row with every order row that shares its customer_id.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join#tb2"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join#f1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s01.b3",
          "role": "syntax",
          "text": "The join condition goes in an ON clause — a boolean expression like those used in WHERE — or in a USING clause when both tables share the same column name. ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id can be written USING (customer_id) when the column name is identical in both tables. INNER is the default: writing JOIN alone, without LEFT, RIGHT, or FULL, already means an INNER JOIN.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join#tb3"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.inner_join#f2",
              "sql.inner_join#f3"
            ]
          }
        },
