    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s06",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.composite_join_conditions"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Composite JOIN conditions",
      "title": "Joining on two columns at once",
      "lede": "When a single column isn't enough to uniquely identify a match, you extend ON with AND.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s06.b1",
          "role": "intro",
          "text": "The shipments table records, for each order_id and product_id pair, the date that item actually shipped. Matching shipments to order_items now needs two columns to agree at once — order_id alone isn't enough, because the same order can contain multiple products.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions#tb1"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions#f1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s06.b2",
          "role": "definition",
          "text": "The ON clause takes a boolean expression of the same kind used in WHERE, so several column-equality tests can be combined with AND to require all of them to match before rows join. FROM order_items AS oi JOIN shipments AS s ON oi.order_id = s.order_id AND oi.product_id = s.product_id joins a shipment row only to the order_items row for the exact same order and the exact same product.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions#tb2"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions#f1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s06.b3",
          "role": "syntax",
          "text": "USING is a shorthand when both tables share the same column names: USING (a, b) expands to ON T1.a = T2.a AND T1.b = T2.b. Since order_items and shipments both use order_id and product_id, that same join can be written FROM order_items JOIN shipments USING (order_id, product_id).",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions#tb3"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.composite_join_conditions#f2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code_walkthrough",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s06.b4",
          "language": "sql",
          "code": "-- ON form: two equality tests combined with AND\nSELECT oi.order_id, oi.product_id, s.shipped_date\nFROM order_items AS oi\nJOIN shipments AS s\n  ON oi.order_id = s.order_id\n AND oi.product_id = s.product_id;\n\n-- USING form: shorthand when column names match on both sides\nSELECT order_id, product_id, s.shipped_date\nFROM order_items AS oi\nJOIN shipments AS s\n  USING (order_id, product_id);",
          "annotations": [
            {
              "start_line": 3,
              "end_line": 6,
              "text": "ON with AND: both order_id AND product_id must match"
            },
            {
              "start_line": 10,
              "end_line": 12,
              "text": "USING: shorthand — expands to the same two-column condition"
            }
          ],
          "reveal": "all",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
