    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s10",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.outer_joins"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Outer Joins",
      "title": "Keeping unmatched rows with LEFT JOIN",
      "lede": "An inner join drops customers with no orders. A LEFT JOIN keeps every customer, filling missing order columns with NULL.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s10.b1",
          "role": "intro",
          "text": "An inner join between customers and orders drops any customer with zero orders. To list every customer — including those who haven't ordered yet — you need an outer join.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.outer_joins"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.outer_joins#tb1"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.outer_joins#f1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s10.b2",
          "role": "definition",
          "text": "A LEFT OUTER JOIN performs an inner join, then adds a row for each row of T1 with no match in T2, filling T2's columns with NULL. SELECT c.name, o.delivery_time_minutes FROM customers AS c LEFT JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id lists every customer; delivery_time_minutes is NULL for any customer with no orders.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.outer_joins"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.outer_joins#tb2"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.outer_joins#f1"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.s10.b3",
          "diagram_kind": "erd",
          "title": "LEFT JOIN vs INNER JOIN",
