      "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
      "title": "Which rows survive: a clear contrast",
      "lede": "Compare what an INNER JOIN keeps versus what it drops.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.r01.b1",
          "role": "example",
          "text": "customers has six rows (customer_id 1–6). orders has rows for customer_id 1, 2, and 3 only. An INNER JOIN ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id produces rows only for customers 1, 2, and 3 — the ones with at least one matching order. Customers 4, 5, and 6 have no matching order row, so they are dropped entirely from the result. The rule: a row survives only if both sides have a match.",
