      "title": "LEFT JOIN vs INNER JOIN: a clear contrast",
      "lede": "The same query with JOIN versus LEFT JOIN gives very different row counts.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_adv_joining_tables.r03.b1",
          "role": "example",
          "text": "INNER JOIN: SELECT c.name, o.delivery_time_minutes FROM customers AS c JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id — returns only customers 1, 2, and 3, who have orders. Customers 4 and 5 are dropped. LEFT JOIN: replace JOIN with LEFT JOIN — now all six customers appear. Customers 4 and 5 get a row with delivery_time_minutes = NULL. The left table (customers) always contributes at least one row to the result.",
